# Review

## Summary

The footer lists all five Relevant Notes entries in the body too. Under this gate, a body link alone is not inflation. An entry is inflated when it also adds no relationship semantics or new framing. Four of the five entries meet that condition.

The four `see-also` entries are inflated. According to the link vocabulary, `see-also` is an escape hatch for cases where the author cannot name a specific reader need. It therefore does not supply the typed relationship that would justify a footer edge beside an inline citation. Each context phrase paraphrases the lens bullet in which the body already cites the same note. A reader of the footer learns nothing about why or when to follow these links beyond what the Current answer section already says.

The Answerability entry is not inflated under this test. It carries a typed label, `evidenced-by`, that the body does not state. That supplies relationship semantics, so the entry passes question 2. Whether that label is accurate is outside this gate.

A possible fix is to give each retained entry a specific label with a reader need, such as `grounds` or `mechanism`, or to drop entries that the inline citations already cover.
